<section class="phero"><img class="phero-bg" src="/assets/img/photos/noreaster.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/noreaster-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/noreaster.webp 1024w" sizes="100vw" width="1024" height="576" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><div class="phero-shade"></div><div class="wrap phero-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><i aria-hidden="true">›</i><a href="/policyholder-guides/">Guides</a><i aria-hidden="true">›</i><span aria-current="page">How Hurricane Deductibles Work on Long Island and in NYC</span></nav><p class="kicker">Guide · 7 min read · October 20, 2026</p><h1>How Hurricane Deductibles Work on Long Island and in NYC</h1><p class="lede">A percentage deductible can take a large bite out of a wind claim. Here is how a hurricane deductible on Long Island or in the city is usually triggered and calculated.</p></div></section>
<div class="wrap layout"><article class="prose">
<h2>Why this deductible surprises people</h2>
<p>Many homeowners never look closely at their hurricane deductible on Long Island or in the five boroughs until a storm has passed and the first payment letter arrives. Unlike a flat $1,000 or $2,500 deductible, a percentage deductible is tied to your dwelling coverage limit, so on a well-insured home the amount you absorb can be far larger than expected.</p>
<p>Percentage hurricane or windstorm deductibles are common in coastal parts of New York, including Nassau and Suffolk counties, New York City and coastal Westchester. They generally range from about 1% to 5% of Coverage A, and they apply only when the event your policy defines actually occurs.</p>

<h2>How the trigger works</h2>
<p>The single most important question is whether the deductible applies at all. Policies define the triggering event in their own words. Common approaches tie the deductible to a hurricane watch or warning issued by the National Weather Service for part of New York, or to a storm that reaches a specified wind speed, with the trigger window running for a set period afterward.</p>
<p>That definition matters because many damaging New York storms are not hurricanes when they arrive. Superstorm Sandy in 2012 led to widespread debate over this very point, and later storms such as Tropical Storm Isaias in 2020 caused heavy tree and wind damage across Long Island and Westchester. A nor’easter or a severe thunderstorm usually falls under the regular deductible, unless the policy defines windstorm more broadly.</p>
<div class="note"><b class="h">Check your declarations</b>Your declarations page should list the hurricane or windstorm deductible as a percentage or dollar amount. The definition of the trigger is usually in an endorsement. Read both, and if the trigger is unclear for your storm, ask the carrier to explain in writing which deductible it applied and why.</div>

<h2>How the math works</h2>
<p>The percentage is applied to the dwelling limit on your declarations page, not to the amount of damage. That is the step most people miss.</p>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Dwelling limit (illustrative)</th><th>2% deductible</th><th>5% deductible</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>$400,000</td><td>$8,000</td><td>$20,000</td></tr>
<tr><td>$650,000</td><td>$13,000</td><td>$32,500</td></tr>
<tr><td>$900,000</td><td>$18,000</td><td>$45,000</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Illustrative example: on a $650,000 dwelling limit with a 2% deductible, a $40,000 roof and siding loss would net about $27,000 before depreciation is considered. Our <a href="/appraisal-calculators/hurricane-deductible-calculator/">hurricane deductible calculator</a> lets you run your own numbers.</p>

<h2>How the deductible affects an appraisal</h2>
<p>Appraisal sets the amount of loss; the carrier then applies the deductible. That has two practical consequences.</p>
<h3>The gap has to clear the deductible</h3>
<p>If the carrier’s estimate is below the deductible, it may pay nothing. If your estimate is well above it, the entire difference matters. Before demanding appraisal, compare both estimates net of the deductible to see what is really at stake.</p>
<h3>Which deductible applies is not an appraisal question</h3>
<p>Whether the hurricane deductible was triggered is generally a coverage and policy-interpretation question, not something the panel decides. If you dispute the deductible itself, raise it with the carrier in writing, consider a complaint to the Department of Financial Services, or speak with a New York attorney.</p>

<h2>Wind versus water after a coastal storm</h2>
<p>Coastal storms bring both wind and surge. Homeowners policies generally exclude flood, including storm surge, so damage from rising water is usually handled under a National Flood Insurance Program or private flood policy, each with its own deductible. Wind damage to a roof, siding or windows falls under the homeowners policy. When both happen to the same South Shore bungalow, the allocation between them can become the largest dispute in the claim.</p>
<ul>
<li>Photograph waterlines, debris lines and roof damage separately</li>
<li>Keep the flood and homeowners claims, adjusters and estimates organized side by side</li>
<li>Note which damage was above the waterline and which was below</li>
</ul>

<h2>Documenting a wind claim</h2>
<p>Because the deductible is large, the quality of the estimate matters even more. A carrier estimate that misses a few slopes of roofing, a run of gutters or damaged siding on the windward side can push the net payment toward zero. After a storm, photograph every elevation of the house, the roof from the ground or a safe vantage point, fallen limbs and any damaged fences or sheds. Keep receipts for tree removal and emergency tarping, which some policies treat separately. If the carrier’s adjuster inspected only part of the property, ask in writing for a reinspection of the rest before deciding whether a dispute over amount remains.</p>

<h2>Before the next storm</h2>
<p>A few minutes now can save frustration later. Find your declarations page, note your dwelling limit and the percentage, and read the endorsement that defines the trigger. If you are reviewing a wind claim from a recent storm, our <a href="/claim-appraisals/hurricane-noreaster-appraisal/">hurricane and nor’easter appraisal</a> page explains how we approach those disputes.</p>
